### 20.2 Key Mitigations

| Risk | Mitigation |
|------|-----------|
| React.lazy breaks route transitions | Add `<Suspense fallback>` with skeleton screens at route group level |
| TanStack Query stale data served | Conservative staleTime (30s for dashboards) + realtime invalidation |
| Zustand store migration breaks clock state | Test clock in/out flow thoroughly; keep global vars as fallback during transition |
| Pagination breaks "total count" displays | Use `count: 'exact'` Supabase option alongside paginated data |
| Service layer adds indirection | TypeScript types ensure compile-time safety; services return same shapes |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The **four quick wins** (lazy loading, clock timer extraction, badge debouncing, and chatbot channel fix) should be executed first as a single coordinated change. Together they deliver ~80% of the total performance improvement with <1 day of effort and near-zero risk. This transforms the application from "prototype under load" to "responsive under moderate use" before the heavier migrations begin.

> [!CAUTION]
> **Do NOT attempt the full TanStack Query migration (P10) before the service layer (P11) is in place.** Without a service layer, you'll be writing query functions inline in 30+ component files, making future refactors extremely painful. The sequence must be: P1-P7 (quick wins) → P11 (service layer) → P10 (TanStack migration) → P8-P9 (query optimization) → P12 (search).
